      "worked": "Since $f$ is a hyperbola, $c\\ne0$ and $ad-bc\\ne0$. Rewrite its rule:\n$$\\begin{align*}f(x)&=\\frac{ax+b}{cx+d}\\\\f(x)&=\\frac{a}{c}+\\frac{bc-ad}{c^2\\left(x+\\frac{d}{c}\\right)}.\\end{align*}$$\nReflect in the $x$-axis and then the $y$-axis:\n$$\\begin{align*}g(x)&=-f(-x)\\\\g(x)&=\\frac{ax-b}{d-cx}\\\\g(x)&=-\\frac{a}{c}+\\frac{bc-ad}{c^2\\left(x-\\frac{d}{c}\\right)}.\\end{align*}$$\nCompare with a translation of $f$:\n$$\\begin{align*}f\\left(x-\\frac{2d}{c}\\right)-\\frac{2a}{c}&=-\\frac{a}{c}+\\frac{bc-ad}{c^2\\left(x-\\frac{d}{c}\\right)}\\\\f\\left(x-\\frac{2d}{c}\\right)-\\frac{2a}{c}&=g(x).\\end{align*}$$\nThus a single translation by $\\left(\\frac{2d}{c},-\\frac{2a}{c}\\right)$ always produces $g$.\n\nFor a purely horizontal translation, the vertical displacement must be zero, so $a=0$; the signed horizontal displacement is $\\frac{2d}{c}$.\n\nFor a purely vertical translation, the horizontal displacement must be zero, so $d=0$; the signed vertical displacement is $-\\frac{2a}{c}$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
